, SessionError>` with `Started { child: std::process::Child, hello: Hello }` attaches first (answered reads `AlreadyRunning`, nothing spawned), spawns `host` as configured and polls every 25 ms until the session answers, the host exits (`HostExited(code)`) or `ready` passes (`Timeout`, the host killed and reaped); it does not check that the answering pid is the child it spawned; `call(state_dir, call: &Call) -> Result<Answer, SessionError>` with `Answer { accepted: bool, json: String }` sends one call and returns its answer — it runs `validate` first, so a call the schema refuses is answered there with its refusal and reaches no session, and every call sent fits the request bound; the answer is read under a 30 s bound on each read (`Timeout` past it; pinned since 2026-10-14-quality-gates by the unix-only `client::imp::tests::an_answer_read_past_its_bound_is_a_timeout`, which reads the bound equal to `wire::ANSWER_BOUND`, 30 s, and a read past a shortened bound on a listener that never answers as `Timeout`), an `oversize` reply reads as `AnswerTooLarge` — the call ran, and what it did is not rolled back — and with no session to reach it reads as `attach` does (packages/escher-driver/src/client.rs:13-261; packages/escher-driver/src/client.rs:263-308). `SessionError` is exactly `AlreadyRunning` · `NoSession` · `Dead` · `HostExited(Option<i32>)` · `Timeout` · `Protocol` · `InvalidLabel` · `StateDirTooLong` · `StateDirNotPrivate` · `Unsupported` · `Io(std::io::ErrorKind)` · `NotSettled(Busy)` · `AnswerTooLarge` · `RecordDirNotPrivate` · `RecordingFailed` — fifteen — with `Display`, `std::error::Error`, `kind() -> &'static str` (one fixed kebab-case word per variant, `already-running` through `recording-failed`), since 2026-10-12-self-description `remedy() -> &'static str`, and `to_json()`; since that chunk each variant's kind, meaning and remedy are one row of a crate-private table, `SESSION_ERRORS: [ErrorSpec; 15]` of `ErrorSpec { kind, meaning, remedy, repeats }` (`repeats` is not served), which `kind()`, `remedy()` and `Display` read; every message is a fixed string — the row's meaning, the `Io` arm appending the kind's name and the `NotSettled` arm the class's — and none carries a path, a label, an id or a value; `StateDirTooLong`'s message states its bound as a figure, at most 94 bytes as given on Linux and 90 on every other target, and its remedy is to name a shorter path for the state directory, or a relative one, which counts only its own bytes; two rows share a remedy, each later row naming the earlier — `dead` repeats `no-session`'s, `answer-too-large` repeats `not-settled`'s (packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:12-224; packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:61-69; packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:79-177). The wire is crate-private and versioned `v3`: one request line and one reply line per connection, `\n`-terminated — requests `hello v3`, `stop v3` and `call v3 <verb> [<name>=<kind>:<value>]…` (kinds `t` text · `n` whole number · `f` flag; the verb, the names and a text value percent-escaped byte for byte — every byte but an ASCII letter, a digit, `-`, `_`, `.` and `~` — and decoded strictly: uppercase hex only, no escape of a byte that needs none, the decoded bytes UTF-8, a number in its one spelling), at most 16,384 bytes where `v1` admitted 64, the widest call the schema admits being 15,360 bytes of values; replies `ok v3 pid={pid} label={label} served={n} idle_expiry_s={s}`, `ok v3 stopping`, `refused version` (a known word with another version) and `refused malformed` (anything else, a call the decoder does not read included), at most 128 bytes — the widest `hello` measures 122 — and, to a call, `ok v3 accepted <json>`, `ok v3 refused <json>`, `ok v3 oversize` or `ok v3 recording-failed` — sent in place of the answer when the host's recording ended after the call, the client reading it as `RecordingFailed` — an answer bounded at 1,048,576 bytes on its JSON line alone and `frame` the one function that turns an answer into its reply; `{n}` counts every request the host answered before this one, refusals included; a refusal or a broken connection changes nothing, an `oversize` reply is no refusal — the call ran and what it did stands — and a `stop` takes effect once its reply is written (packages/escher-driver/src/wire.rs:12-303). On a non-unix target `serve`, `start`, `attach`, `stop` and — for a call the schema admits — `call` return `Unsupported`, and so does `Session::recording_into`; a `Session` held in process without a recording works on every target. The command and refusal schema is stated once, as `'static` data; one `Session` method takes a `Call` — `run`, which executes it (below) — and since 2026-10-10-driver-cli a call reaches it from outside the process too: a host decodes a `call` request and hands it to `run`, and the client's `call` and the command line check it with `validate` before anything is sent. `VERBS` lists eleven verbs in three levels (`Level` is `Instance` · `Session` · `Tool`, its `name()` the words `instance` · `session` · `tool`; the third level since 2026-10-12-self-description, packages/escher-driver/src/schema/spec.rs:129-136) — first the seven asked of the held instance, in order: `snapshot` (no argument; result `text`), `click` (`id`), `type` (`id`, `text`), `press` (`key`, optional `shift`, false when absent), `advance` (`ms`), `scroll` (`id`), the sixth joined at 2026-10-07-refusal-detection as a widening of the validated verb set, ratified by the founder (2026-10-07), and, since 2026-10-12-headless-screenshot, `screenshot` (one optional `id`, `ArgKind::Id`; result `width` (count), `height` (count) and `png` (`FieldKind::Png`, base64 text), all always present), whose help states one measured limit — an element shot is cropped to the part of its bounds that can be seen — the overlay scrollbar fade reading the session's clock, so two shots of one state with no `advance` between them read the same pixels (ratified by the founder, 2026-10-18) (packages/escher-driver/src/schema.rs:275-307); then the three asked of the session itself, joined at 2026-10-10-driver-cli as a further widening of that set, ratified by the founder (2026-10-10): `start` (`app`; result `label`, `pid`, `idle_expiry_s`), `status` (no argument; result those three and `served`) and `stop` (no argument; result `stopped`); then, since 2026-10-12-self-description, the one asked of the tool itself, a further widening of that set ratified by the founder (2026-10-12, «help — десятый глагол», by question dialog in the overseer session, relayed verbatim by the overseer): `help` (no argument; result `verbs` · `node_keys` · `key_names` · `busy_classes` · `causes` · `exit_statuses` · `session_errors`, each field with its help; packages/escher-driver/src/schema.rs:372-428) — each a `VerbSpec { name, level, help, args, fields }` of `ArgSpec { name, kind, required, help }` and `FieldSpec { name, kind, always, help }`, found by `verb(name) -> Option<&'static VerbSpec>` on its exact name only. `ArgKind` is `Id` (1 to `MAX_ID_BYTES` = 1024 bytes of text) · `Text` (0 to `MAX_TEXT_BYTES` = 4096 bytes) · `Key` (one name of `KEY_NAMES`: `tab` · `enter` · `escape` · `backspace` · `delete` · `space` · `arrow-up` · `arrow-down` · `arrow-left` · `arrow-right` · `home` · `end`) · `Flag` · `Milliseconds` (a whole number, 1 to `MAX_MILLISECONDS` = 60000) · `Name` (1 to 32 bytes of `a-z`, `0-9` and `-` — `start`'s `app`, the sixth kind, since 2026-10-10-driver-cli). The five acting verbs' result fields are `settled` (flag), `busy` (one of `BUSY_CLASSES` — `render` · `layout` · `loads` — present only when `settled` is false), `added` and `changed` (nodes, each keyed by the thirteen keys `NODE_FIELDS` names: `id` · `parent` · `role` · `name` · `enabled` · `checked` · `value` · `selected` · `pressed` · `invalid` · `focused` · `bounds` · `differs` — nine until 2026-10-10-snapshot-states added the three after `value`, the constant's type moving from `[&str; 9]` to `[&str; 12]` with no verb, argument kind, field kind, refusal cause or `SessionError` added, and twelve until 2026-10-12-self-description added `differs`, written on a changed node only: the keys whose reading differs from before the step, in this order, a node that only moved reading `bounds` alone; since that chunk the constant is `[NodeFieldSpec; 13]`, each row `NodeFieldSpec { name, help }` whose help states the key's reading — `role` and `name` as the accessibility tree reads them, `enabled` following the attribute's presence whatever its value, `value` the fixed mask for a password or a file input, `checked` a native checkbox's or radio's own state, else the toggle of the five checkable roles (`aria-checked`), a mixed one reading false, `selected` · `pressed` · `invalid` read by `aria-selected` · `aria-pressed` (left out for those five roles) · `aria-invalid` with the boolean model's two limits (a mixed toggle reads `pressed` false, a grammar or spelling error reads `invalid` true), `bounds` the same however far a box's content is scrolled; packages/escher-driver/src/schema.rs:36-115) and `removed` (ids); `advance` adds `advanced_ms` and `scroll` adds `in_view` (a flag, always present: whether the element is in view after the step); `FieldKind` is `Text` · `Flag` · `Milliseconds` · `Busy` · `Ids` · `Nodes` · `Count`, the seventh the kind of the session-level fields `pid`, `served` and `idle_expiry_s` (`label` is text, `stopped` a flag), then `Words` · `Entries`, the kinds of `help`'s fields since 2026-10-12-self-description, then `Png`, the kind of `screenshot`'s `png` field (base64 text), since 2026-10-12-headless-screenshot — ten kinds. The command line's four exit statuses are a table of the schema since that chunk: `EXIT_STATUSES: [ExitStatus; 4]` of `ExitStatus { status, name, meaning }` — `0` `accepted` · `1` `refused` · `2` `usage` · `3` `session-error`, the meanings the `command_line` doc comment stated, `usage` reading a `--session` that is absent where the verb needs one or is given twice — which the command line's status constants read and `help` serves (packages/escher-driver/src/schema.rs:436-474). `describe() -> String` (since 2026-10-12-self-description) writes the tool's self-description, the answer of `help`, as one line of JSON through the crate's `json` writer: its keys are `HELP`'s seven result fields in order, every key name read from `HELP.fields` and every text from the tables — `VERBS`, `NODE_FIELDS`, `KEY_NAMES`, `BUSY_CLASSES`, `CAUSES` with their remedies, `EXIT_STATUSES` and the session errors with their remedies: 11 verbs, 13 node keys, 12 key names, 3 busy classes, 9 causes, 4 exit statuses, 15 session errors — reading no environment and printing nothing; its callers are the command line's `help` and the MCP server's `help` tool, which serve the same bytes — the program's own fixed `'static` text of the one table riding the ratified carriers, ratified by the founder (2026-10-12, «Внутри, с правилом», by question dialog in the overseer session, relayed verbatim by the overseer) (packages/escher-driver/src/describe.rs:11-58). A step that ran and did not go quiet is a result — `settled: false` with its class — not a refusal. `advance` moves the session's clock forward by `ms` milliseconds on every session — the engine's animations, the overlay scrollbar fade and, through the step a caller hands over with `Session::with_time` where there is one, the app's own time together — then settles; two `click`s on one point with no `advance` of at least 500 ms between them read as a double click, as `click`'s help says (ratified by the founder, 2026-10-18). `validate(call: &Call) -> Result<Command, Refusal>` takes a `Call { verb: String, args: Vec<(String, ArgValue)> }` (`ArgValue` is `Text(String)` · `Number(i64)` · `Flag(bool)`) and receives no `Session`, so a refused call changes nothing; it answers once per call, the first failure in this order: the verb by exact name (absent → `unknown-verb`; so does a session-level verb or, since 2026-10-12-self-description, the tool-level `help`, refused before its arguments are read, and a verb the table lists in a shape no `Command` has), then the call's arguments in the order passed (a name the verb lacks → `malformed` with `Fault::Unnamed`; a name passed before → `Repeated`), then the verb's arguments in the schema's order (required and absent → `Missing`; a value of another kind → `WrongKind`; a value outside its bound, or a text that is no key name → `OutOfBound`). `Command` is exactly `Snapshot` · `Click { id }` · `Type { id, text }` · `Press { key, shift }` · `Advance { ms: u32 }` · `Scroll { id }` · `Screenshot { id: Option<String> }` (the last since 2026-10-12-headless-screenshot), with `spec() -> &'static VerbSpec`; the session-level check is `validate_session(call: &Call) -> Result<SessionCommand, Refusal>` — `validate`'s rules and order over the three session-level rows and, since 2026-10-12-self-description, the tool-level `help`, an instance-level verb reading `unknown-verb` there and an argument to `help` `malformed` with `Fault::Unnamed` — with `SessionCommand` exactly `Start { app }` · `Status` · `Stop` · `Help` and a `spec()` of its own (`Help`'s is `HELP`); `Key` has one variant per name of `KEY_NAMES` (`ALL`, `name`, `from_name`). `Refusal` holds a `Cause` and, only beside `malformed`, a `Fault` (private fields; `new(cause)`, `malformed(fault)`, `cause()`, `fault()`; `Display` as `{name}: {remedy}` or `{name} ({fault}): {remedy}`; `std::error::Error`). `Cause` is closed at eight, listed in `CAUSES` (`[Cause; 8]`, ratified by the founder, 2026-10-18) — `unknown-verb` · `malformed` · `not-found` · `stale` · `disabled` · `covered` · `off-screen` · `fonts-unavailable`, the eighth (meaning "the app this session holds has no font it can paint text with", remedy "boot the app with a font it can decode") — each with a fixed `name()`, `meaning()` and `remedy()`; `Fault` is `Missing` · `Unnamed` · `Repeated` · `WrongKind` · `OutOfBound`, a variant that names an argument holding the schema's own name for it; none of the three has a `String` field, so a refusal holds nothing a call supplied. All eight are returned by code: `validate` returns `unknown-verb` and `malformed` only, and the executor returns the other six — `not-found`, `stale`, `disabled`, `covered`, `off-screen` and `fonts-unavailable`, the last from `screenshot` alone, checked before anything is painted (below). Two texts were reworded at 2026-10-07-refusal-detection, both `off-screen`'s and each on a recorded word, no other cause text moving: its meaning reads "the element lies outside the viewport, or outside the visible part of a scrolling box that holds it; a scrolling box under a transform is read where it stands untransformed, so an element inside it can read `off-screen` though it shows" — its first clause the operator's (2026-10-07, at that chunk's plan review), its second a measured limit (the Scrolling, selection, tree contract) (packages/escher-driver/src/refusal.rs:25-28; packages/escher-driver/src/refusal.rs:81-86) and its remedy "the element is out of view: bring it into view with `scroll`, then act again" (the founder, 2026-10-07, relayed verbatim by the overseer). At 2026-10-10-driver-cli four more texts were amended so that the tool itself said two measured limits and what `type` does (the operator, 2026-10-10, at that chunk's plan review: a measured limit is stated where an agent reads the tool), and at 2026-10-10-scrolling-box-bounds-and-hit, both limits fixed in the engine, three of the four were restated, the cause set — eight, in its order — and every other text standing: `covered`'s meaning reads "another element is hit at the point the action would land; a box clipped on one axis stops a hit at its whole padding box, so an element lying outside it along the axis it does not clip can read `covered` though it shows; a box with no width or no height is hit at its centre on the element that holds it